        {
          "id": "fact_001",
          "title": "Church of St Vincent de Paul: modern sacred architecture in Pošeň",
          "text": "The Church of St Vincent de Paul is a notable example of modern Slovak sacred architecture in Bratislava's Ružinov district. Its foundation stone was laid on 22 April 1990, and the building was completed and consecrated on 16 September 2000. The principal architect was Eduard Šutek. The altar, baptismal font and tabernacle were created by sculptor Viliam Loviška in 2001–2002; Jozef Baus, Juraj Vittek and icon painter Ladislav Németh contributed to the artistic design of the altar painting. The city's heritage inventory describes the church as a representative example of modern Slovak architecture in Ružinov. The parish gives its current address as Tomášikova 8, 821 03 Bratislava."
        },
        {
          "id": "fact_002",
          "title": "Pošeň and the vanished Mlynské branch: the landscape before the housing estate",
          "text": "According to Ružinov's oldest available map from 1781, the area of today's Pošeň consisted mainly of fields, meadows and sparse woodland. A few farmsteads clustered along the Mlynské branch canal, locally known as Smradľavka, which connected the Little Danube with the area of today's Bratislava harbour. The canal roughly followed the present boundary between Pošeň, Trávniky and Prievoz; parts of its former course are now followed by the busy Tomášikova and Gagarinova roads. A local source also records the canal overflowing during the 1947 flood. The former agricultural landscape included a farm known as Heckler's Court. Its transformation into a residential district began in the mid-1960s: the first residents moved into new flats in 1966–1967, and 3,105 flats had been built by 1969. Pošeň therefore preserves the story of both a vanished water landscape and rapid post-war urbanisation."
