    "en": {
      "representative_place": "Nivy – the Klinger workers’ colony and former industrial district",
      "facts": [
        {
          "id": "fact_001",
          "title": "Klingerka – the jute and hemp factory that shaped Nivy",
          "text": "Klingerka began operating in the industrial district of Nivy in 1888, through the initiative of Austrian entrepreneur Heinrich Klinger. It produced goods made from flax, hemp and jute, including wallpaper, imitation leather, oilcloth, waterproof sacks and, later, sailcloth, hoses and military equipment. Production expanded in 1897. The factory became one of the district’s major employers; a regional industrial-heritage study records 600–700 workers and as many as 1,000 before the First World War. Production ended in 1939. The modern neighbourhood recalls the factory through the name Klingerka and a jute-weave design, but the original factory buildings have not survived."
        },
        {
          "id": "fact_002",
          "title": "Klinger workers’ colony – factory housing with gardens",
          "text": "As the textile factory expanded, it needed housing for its workforce. In 1894, the Klinger workers’ colony was built nearby. Apartment houses on Plátennícka, Súkennícka and Valchárska streets formed an enclosed residential area with green courtyards and gardens. The flats were very modest: typically an entrance hall, toilet, kitchen and one room, often without a private bathroom; workers used showers at the factory. The colony also included small family houses on nearby streets. The surviving parts of the original housing are mainly the houses on Alešova, Čipkárska and Gusevova streets."
        },
        {
          "id": "fact_003",
          "title": "Mlynské nivy – a vanished Danube channel and island of mills",
          "text": "The name Mlynské nivy preserves the memory of a landscape that existed before Bratislava’s industrial transformation. The Malé Novozámocké arm of the Danube, later called the Mlynský arm, flowed through this area. Its course formed Mlynský Island, where watermills stood. A wooden bridge near today’s Košická Street connected important local routes. As the Danube was regulated, side channels were filled and the railway was built in the late 19th century, the river landscape gave way to factories and warehouses. Within a few decades, Mlynské nivy changed from a wetland and milling landscape into one of the city’s principal industrial districts."
        },
        {
          "id": "fact_004",
          "title": "Winter Harbour – a safe haven for ships and Bratislava’s industrial gateway",
          "text": "Construction of Bratislava’s Winter Harbour began in 1897 after major engineering works on the Danube. Initially intended as a safe winter refuge for vessels, it was ultimately designed to accommodate up to 200 ships and became an important centre of Danube trade. After the First World War it developed into a modern cargo port, connected to factories and warehouses by railway sidings. From 1923 to 1942 it hosted the Oriental Market, which aimed to strengthen Bratislava’s international trade links. Allied air raids severely damaged the harbour in 1944–1945. After the river was cleared of explosives and the facilities rebuilt, cargo handling resumed in 1948. River freight declined after 1989, but the harbour basins and surviving technical infrastructure remain an important part of the city’s industrial memory."
        }
      ]
    },
